## Issue / fix: fix(goose-server): cache TLS cert to disk to avoid slow startup on first launch

## Description
## Summary

On fresh macOS installs, `rcgen` TLS key generation takes 2–8 seconds. Combined with Electron startup overhead, this regularly exceeded the 10-second health-check timeout in `goosed.ts`, causing the desktop app to fail on first launch while working fine on relaunches (fixes #8137).

**Changes:**
- `crates/goose-server/src/tls.rs`: adds `get_tls_cache_dir()`, `load_cached_tls()`, and `save_tls_to_cache()` — caches cert + key + fingerprint to `~/.config/goose/tls/` on first generation; subsequent startups skip `rcgen` entirely. Cache miss silently falls back to fresh generation.
- `ui/desktop/src/goosed.ts`: raises `checkServerStatus` timeout from 10 s to 30 s as a belt-and-suspenders guard for the unavoidable first-launch case.

**No new dependencies introduced** — uses only `std::fs`, `std::path`, and dependencies already present in `Cargo.toml`.

## Test plan

- [ ] `cargo test --package goose-server tls` — existing TLS tests pass
- [ ] Fresh install on macOS (delete `~/.config/goose/tls/` to simulate): desktop app starts successfully within 30 s
- [ ] Second launch: app starts in < 1 s (cert loaded from cache, no rcgen invocation)
- [ ] Corrupt cache file (`echo bad > ~/.config/goose/tls/server.pem`): app still starts (falls back to fresh generation)

